#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""session-write — the FOUR write verbs, layered on `session-resolve`.

    session-write dismiss <selector> [--client TTY]
    session-write focus   <selector> --i-am-at-the-keyboard [--stay] [--client TTY]
    session-write type    <selector> --text "..." [--append]
    session-write send    <selector> [--text "..."] [--append] [--allow-shell-exec]

`session-resolve` is READ-ONLY BY CONSTRUCTION and STAYS THAT WAY. This module
does not extend it, does not weaken `_assert_read_only`, and does not add a verb
to `TMUX_READ_ONLY_SUBCOMMANDS`. It CONSUMES its resolution — importing it
in-process (it has no `.py` extension, so by `SourceFileLoader`) and reusing its
parsers, its constants and its argv segmenter — and owns a SEPARATE seam with a
SEPARATE allowlist. `test_session_write.py` pins both halves: that this file
never grows a read-only violation, and that session-resolve's allowlist is
still exactly three read verbs.


🔴 WHY A WRAPPER AT ALL, AND WHAT IT WOULD MEAN TO GET IT WRONG
---------------------------------------------------------------
The alternative design was a permission-allowlist entry of the shape
`Bash(tmux send-keys:*)`. That is a TOTAL BYPASS of the Bash permission
classifier, not a loosening of it: the classifier reads the string
`tmux send-keys -t %81 -l -- <payload>`, approves the *tmux invocation*, and the
payload then executes in a DIFFERENT pane having never been classified at all.
Whatever that pane's shell does with it is outside every gate in the system.

So the single intended allowlist entry is `Bash(scripts/session-write:*)`, and
the entire value of that entry is that THIS FILE constrains the payload. A
revision that ends up handing arbitrary argv text to `send-keys` with no
validation has not implemented the wrapper — it has rebuilt the hole. Every
guard below exists to make that concrete, and each is enforced in code and
mutation-tested rather than asserted in a comment.


🔴 MEASURED REFUTATIONS OF THIS PR'S OWN BRIEF — READ BEFORE CHANGING A GATE
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Five things the brief asserted are wrong on this host. Each was measured, and
the code below follows the measurement, not the brief.

1. `runtime` DOES NOT SEPARATE "Claude pane" FROM "shell pane". It has THREE
   states, and the third is the one that matters. Measured 2026-08-19 over all
   92 resolved targets on the workbench:

       runtime: claude=51   opencode=5   None=36

   session-manager's own source says why, at the `kind` vs `runtime` block:
   `runtime` comes from the LEDGER record and is "NULL whenever no writer has
   recorded that window, which is the common case for a session that has not
   taken a turn yet." So `None` means UNRECORDED, not "shell" — 39% of targets,
   and a real Claude pane that has not taken a turn sits in it.

   The pane-level discriminator session-manager actually uses for claude/shell
   is `row_class`, i.e. `row.get("claude")` (`pane_current_command =~ /claude/`).
   🔴 THAT FIELD IS NOT REACHABLE FROM A RESOLVED TARGET. `session-resolve`
   declares `claude` in `SM_PASSTHROUGH_FIELDS` and collects it into `sm_block`,
   but `build_targets` never emits it — measured, `'claude' in target` is False
   for all 92. Fixing that belongs in session-resolve, not here.

   So `AGENT_RUNTIMES` is an ALLOWLIST of the two runtimes measured to be agent
   harnesses, and everything else — `None` included — falls to the guarded side.
   Failing toward "treat it as a shell" is the safe direction: the cost is one
   explicit `--allow-shell-exec` flag on a pane that turned out to be Claude,
   versus executing an agent-authored line in a live shell.

2. THERE IS NOT ONE BASE TERMINAL. Measured at the same instant, `list-clients`
   reported TWO:

       /dev/pts/0   alacritty  312x63  session=datapacket-talos    flags=[attached, focused, UTF-8]
       /dev/pts/69  alacritty  77x64   session=datapacket-talos-3  flags=[attached, UTF-8]

   and `compute_visibility` publishes `base_terminal = base_all[0]` — THE FIRST
   ONE IN LIST ORDER, not the focused one. It happens to be the focused one
   today, which is exactly why a test written against that field would pass now
   and steer `switch-client` at the wrong terminal later. This module therefore
   does NOT read `visibility.base_terminal`; it re-derives from the client list
   and disambiguates on the `focused` FLAG, which was unique among base
   terminals in the measurement (the two popups also carry `focused`, so the
   flag is only a discriminator WITHIN the base-terminal set).

3. `switch-client` NEEDS `-c`. With two attached base terminals, a bare
   `switch-client -t <session>` acts on tmux's notion of the current client,
   which is not a thing this process has. Every client-directed verb here names
   its client explicitly.

4. THE POPUP PAIR IN THE BRIEF IS STALE (it named scratch11 + scratch14).
   Measured: scratch11 at /dev/pts/2 and scratch13 at /dev/pts/6. The COUNT is
   what the design turns on and the count was right — two, hence ambiguous by
   default — but nothing here hardcodes either session name.

5. THE TARGET IN THE BRIEF IS NOT VISIBLE AND NOT COVERED. `scratch2:@81`
   measured `visible=False, covered=False, attached_clients=[]`: no client is
   attached to `scratch2` at all. That does not change any verb, but it means
   `covered` is NOT the precondition for `dismiss` — see the dismiss section.


🔴 THREE tmux BEHAVIOURS THAT SILENTLY CORRUPT — OR EXECUTE — THE PAYLOAD
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Measured on tmux 3.7b in a PRIVATE `-L` server (never the operator's), because
all three are payload-integrity bugs that no amount of reading the man page
reveals. (c) was found by an adversarial audit AFTER the first version of this
file shipped a denylist, and it is the reason `--text` is now an ALLOWLIST.

  a) A TRAILING `;` ON THE TEXT ARGUMENT IS EATEN.

         tmux send-keys -t %0 -l -- 'abc;'   ->  rc 0, pane received "abc"

     tmux separates commands on a `;` WELDED to the end of an argv token (this
     is the same fact `session-resolve._split_on_separators` was built around).
     As the last argv element it starts an empty command, so nothing dangerous
     runs — but the operator's semicolon is GONE and the call reports success.
     Silent truncation of a payload is precisely the class of bug this wrapper
     exists to prevent, so `--text` ending in `;` is REFUSED with the remedy
     (append a space) rather than mutated behind the caller's back.

  b) A NEWLINE INSIDE `-l` LITERAL TEXT IS A REAL ENTER — IT SUBMITS.

         tmux send-keys -t %0 -l -- $'X\nY'  ->  pane received X, ENTER, Y

     `type` promises "literal, no Enter". A `\n` in the text breaks that promise
     without any error, and on a shell pane it EXECUTES. `\r` behaves the same.
     Both verbs refuse `\n`/`\r` in `--text`; `send` presses Enter with its own
     separate `send-keys <pane> Enter` argv, so the submit is one auditable act
     rather than a character hidden inside a payload.

  c) 🔴 ENTER IS NOT THE ONLY EXECUTION EVENT. A CONTROL CHARACTER EXECUTES.

         tmux send-keys -t %0 -l -- $'touch /tmp/d/EXECUTED\x0f'   # NO Enter
             ->  /tmp/d/EXECUTED was CREATED
         same payload without the \x0f, still no Enter
             ->  nothing ran      (negative control: the rig discriminates)

     `\x0f` is Ctrl-O, readline's `operate-and-get-next` — a BASH DEFAULT, not
     an exotic rebinding. `\x1ba` is zsh emacs `accept-and-hold`. Any keymap
     entry bound to an accept-line variant is another one, and the set is a
     property of the OPERATOR'S shell config, which this file cannot read.

     🔴 This REFUTES the premise the first version of this file was built on —
     that "`type` cannot execute because it never sends Enter". It can. The
     first version enforced a DENYLIST of exactly NUL/`\n`/`\r`/trailing-`;`,
     so `\x0f` sailed through and `session-write type` was an arbitrary-command
     executor in any shell pane: precisely the Bash-permission-classifier
     bypass this wrapper exists to prevent.

     Adding `\x0f` to the denylist would have been the same mistake one
     codepoint later — the next keybinding walks it. `--text` is therefore
     validated against an ALLOWLIST (printable characters plus tab); every
     other codepoint is refused BY NUMBER. NUL, `\n` and `\r` are non-printable
     too, so the allowlist is what actually refuses them; their named messages
     survive only because a specific diagnosis beats a generic one.


THE GUARDS, AND WHY EACH IS SHAPED THAT WAY
--------------------------------------------
G1  RE-RESOLVE AT WRITE TIME. No verb accepts a tmux address from argv. argv
    carries a SELECTOR; this module resolves it and refuses anything that is not
    `resolved` (ambiguous -> EXIT_AMBIGUOUS, unmatched -> EXIT_UNMATCHED, the
    same codes session-resolve's own CLI uses). Handing `%81` straight through
    would mean tmux, not this file, decides what gets written to.

G2  LOCAL HOST ONLY, AND THE HIGHEST-CONSEQUENCE GUARD HERE. session-resolve
    resolves across BOTH hosts via session-manager; tmux here is local-only.
    A laptop-resolved `scratch2:@81` handed to local tmux does not fail — it
    writes to whatever LOCAL window happens to share that address. Measured, 30
    of 92 targets are on the laptop, so this is a live, reachable state and not
    a hypothetical. The local host comes from session-resolve's own authority
    chain, `coverage.registry.local_host`, and an UNKNOWN local host is refused
    too: locality that cannot be proven is not locality.

G3  RE-VERIFY IMMEDIATELY BEFORE THE WRITE, not at resolution time. Resolution
    reads four sources and can take seconds; a window can close in that gap.
    `reverify` re-reads tmux and requires the exact `(session, window_id)` still
    to exist and — for pane-directed verbs — the exact `pane_id`. The FRESH
    client list is also what the client-selecting verbs choose from, because
    "re-verify at the moment you ACT" applies to the popup you are about to
    detach just as much as to the window you are about to write to.

G4  A WRITE-SUBCOMMAND ALLOWLIST AT THE SUBPROCESS SEAM. `_assert_allowed`
    mirrors session-resolve's discipline in the other direction: an allowlist,
    not a denylist, checked per tmux COMMAND (segmented with session-resolve's
    own `_split_on_separators`, so a welded `;` cannot smuggle a second one
    past). `kill-session`, `kill-pane`, `respawn-pane` cannot leave this file.

G5  CHANGING THE OPERATOR'S SCREEN IS A pkill-CLASS ACTION. BOTH `focus` and
    `dismiss` refuse without `--i-am-at-the-keyboard`, and neither is ever a
    side effect of another verb — no other verb constructs a `select-window`,
    `switch-client` or `detach-client` argv at all.

    🔴 `dismiss` was NOT behind this gate in the first version, on the reading
    that taking an overlay DOWN is not the same as raising a window. That is
    wrong twice: RULES.md names the hazard as "changes what a human is looking
    at RIGHT NOW", which a disappearing overlay does; and see G6 — the client
    it detaches is not provably an overlay at all.

G6  DISMISS NEVER DETACHES A BASE TERMINAL. That would drop the operator out of
    tmux entirely. Note `popup` and `base_terminal` are exact complements in
    `session-resolve.parse_clients`, so a chooser that only ever looks at popups
    makes this guard UNREACHABLE — which is a guard that cannot be tested. So
    `--client` resolves against ALL attached clients and the assertion runs
    afterwards, on the single line every dismiss path funnels through.

    🔴 BUT "POPUP" IS A HEURISTIC, AND IT CANNOT PROVE AN OVERLAY.
    `session-resolve` classifies any client whose TERM starts with `tmux` as a
    popup. That is sound for the clients `display-popup -E "tmux attach …"`
    creates — measured, they report `tmux-256color` — but it is a statement
    about TERM, not about provenance. A client that is a genuine nested
    `tmux attach` reports the same TERM and would be detached as though it were
    an overlay, dropping a REAL session.

    Measured, tmux 3.7b, private `-L` server — how reachable that actually is:
        same server, `$TMUX` set   -> tmux REFUSES ("sessions should be nested
                                      with care, unset $TMUX to force")
        a DIFFERENT tmux server    -> ATTACHES, TERM `tmux-256color`
        `$TMUX` unset, forced      -> attaches
    So it is not "any nested attach" — the ordinary case is refused by tmux
    itself. It takes a second server or a deliberate `unset TMUX`. That is a
    narrow residual, not a common one, but it is not zero: hence G5 over
    `dismiss`, and hence every dismiss result SAYS which client it detached and
    that the classification was made on TERM.

G7  AMBIGUITY IS REFUSED, NEVER GUESSED — matching session-resolve's own stance
    on multiple matches. Two popups (the measured state) or two focused base
    terminals is a refusal naming the candidates, not a coin flip.

G8  THE SHELL-EXECUTION GATE, on `send` only — `send` presses Enter, and on a
    shell that runs a command while on an agent runtime it submits a prompt to
    a harness a human will read. See the three-state reading in (1).

    🔴 THE SCOPE OF THIS GATE RESTS ON G13, NOT ON "type NEVER SENDS ENTER".
    The first version justified leaving `type` ungated by asserting that Enter
    is the only execution event. Measurement (c) refuted that: a Ctrl-O in the
    payload executed the pane's buffer with no Enter sent. `type` is ungated
    because the G13 ALLOWLIST makes an execution event unrepresentable in
    `--text` — not because of anything intrinsic to `send-keys -l`. The two are
    ONE mechanism: weaken G13 and `type` silently becomes a shell executor
    again. `test_type_is_ungated_ONLY_because_the_allowlist_makes_it_safe` pins
    that relationship rather than either half alone.

G9  THE UNSENT-PROMPT GATE. `unsent_prompt` is text the operator typed and never
    sent. Writing into that pane CONCATENATES onto it — the agent's text fuses
    with a human's half-finished sentence and, on `send`, submits the pair.
    `--append` is the acknowledgement. 🔴 The prompt's CONTENT is never printed
    and never logged: this repo is PUBLIC and that string is captured text off
    the operator's screen (session-resolve carries the same rule at
    `SIGNAL_CAPTURED_TEXT_KEY`). Only its presence and length leave this file.

    🔴 BARE `send` IS GATED TOO, and in the first version it was not. The
    reading then was that submitting text already in the pane is bare send's
    only purpose, so gating it would refuse the whole verb. But the text
    already in the pane is exactly what G9 is about: `unsent_prompt` is a
    HUMAN's half-finished sentence, and bare `send` submits it verbatim with
    nobody having read it. That is the same hazard the `--text` path is gated
    for, arriving by a shorter route. `--append` acknowledges it either way.

G13 THE `--text` ALLOWLIST — printable characters plus tab, everything else
    refused BY CODEPOINT. See measurement (c): this is the guard that makes
    `type` safe, and a denylist provably could not.

G14 THE LOG PATH IS CONFINED TO ONE ROOT. `--log-path` and `$SESSION_WRITE_LOG`
    name a file this process CREATES (with `makedirs`) and APPENDS
    caller-influenced JSON to, and both are reachable from the single
    `Bash(scripts/session-write:*)` allowlist entry — an unconstrained
    create-and-append anywhere on the filesystem, from an entry granted on the
    premise that this file constrains what it does. Both are now resolved with
    `realpath` and refused unless they land under `LOG_ROOT`.

G10 EVERY WRITE IS LOGGED, including refusals — a refusal is the interesting
    record. See LOG_PATH below.


🔴 FOCUS: RECORD BEFORE YOU RAISE, RESTORE BEFORE YOU FINISH
--------------------------------------------------------------
Both dimensions RULES.md names are covered, and they are DIFFERENT dimensions:

    the client's SESSION   — restored with `switch-client -c <tty> -t <session>`
    the session's WINDOW   — restored with `select-window -t <session>:<@id>`

Measured on this host, the base terminal is attached to `datapacket-talos` while
the brief's target is in `scratch2`, so the `switch-client` path is the COMMON
case, not an edge one.

🔴 THE i3 WORKSPACE IS OUT OF SCOPE, EXPLICITLY. This module is tmux-only and
issues no `i3-msg`, no `xdotool`, no `wmctrl`. If the operator's alacritty is on
another i3 workspace, focusing a tmux window here does NOT bring it on screen
and this module CANNOT restore an i3 workspace it never changed. Every focus
result says so in as many words rather than leaving the scope of the restore to
be inferred.

Restore is the DEFAULT and `--stay` opts out. That is deliberately the opposite
of what a human typing `focus` wants, and the tension is real: an operator
asking to be shown a window wants `--stay`; an agent raising a window
transiently must put the screen back. The default serves the caller this
permission entry exists for. A restore that FAILS is reported and returns
`EXIT_RESTORE_FAILED` — the screen is then somewhere the operator did not put
it, which is the one outcome a caller must never learn about by silence.

At most ONE raise per run, and no verb loops. The raise/restore pair is two
switches by construction, which is what "record then restore" mandates; it is
not the ping-pong the rule forbids.


LOGGING
-------
One JSON object per line at `~/.claude/session-write.log` (override:
`$SESSION_WRITE_LOG`, or `--log-path`), beside the existing
`~/.claude/claude-notify.log` rather than inventing a second convention. The
path resolves at CALL time, never as a dataclass field default — session-resolve
has a scar from exactly that (`registry_dir` bound at class-creation time sailed
past a monkeypatch and read 48 live records during a "hermetic" test run).

The log is NOT a committed artifact and IS expected to contain the text written
by `type`/`send`, because an audit log that omits the payload cannot answer the
only question it exists for. It never contains `unsent_prompt` content (G9).
A log write that fails NEVER blocks or fails a verb — it is reported on stderr;
a wrapper that refuses to work because it cannot journal is worse than the gap.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import datetime as _dt
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import subprocess
import sys
import unicodedata
from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from typing import Any, Callable, Optional

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Import session-resolve IN-PROCESS. It has no `.py` extension.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
_HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
SESSION_RESOLVE_PATH = os.path.join(_HERE, "session-resolve")
#: 🔴 The name is shared with `test_session_resolve.py` ON PURPOSE. Loading the
#: same file twice under two names yields two module objects whose dataclasses
#: are DIFFERENT CLASSES, so an `sr.Sources` built by one is not the `Sources`
#: the other's functions type-check against. Reusing the entry keeps one.
SESSION_RESOLVE_MODULE = "session_resolve"


def _load_session_resolve(path: str = SESSION_RESOLVE_PATH):
    existing = sys.modules.get(SESSION_RESOLVE_MODULE)
    if existing is not None:
        return existing
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(
        SESSION_RESOLVE_MODULE,
        importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(SESSION_RESOLVE_MODULE, path))
    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    # Register BEFORE exec_module — `@dataclass` resolves its own class's module
    # through `sys.modules[cls.__module__]`.
    sys.modules[SESSION_RESOLVE_MODULE] = module
    spec.loader.exec_module(module)
    return module


sr = _load_session_resolve()


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Constants
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
#: 🔴 The ONLY tmux WRITE subcommands this module may ever run. An allowlist, so
#: a verb nobody thought to ban still fails closed. `kill-session`, `kill-pane`,
#: `respawn-pane`, `kill-window`, `rename-session` are absent and unreachable.
TMUX_WRITE_SUBCOMMANDS = ("send-keys", "select-window", "switch-client",
                          "detach-client")

#: The reads this module performs for G3. 🔴 DERIVED from session-resolve's own
#: tuple rather than retyped: one rule, one place, and a read verb added there
#: cannot leave the two lists disagreeing about what "a read" is.
TMUX_REVERIFY_SUBCOMMANDS = tuple(sr.TMUX_READ_ONLY_SUBCOMMANDS)

TMUX_ALLOWED_SUBCOMMANDS = TMUX_WRITE_SUBCOMMANDS + TMUX_REVERIFY_SUBCOMMANDS

#: 🔴 The runtimes measured to be agent harnesses. An ALLOWLIST — see refutation
#: (1): `runtime` is a THREE-state field and `None` (36 of 92 targets) means
#: "no ledger record", not "shell". Everything not named here is treated as the
#: higher-risk case.
AGENT_RUNTIMES = ("claude", "opencode")

#: The tmux client flag that distinguishes the base terminal the operator is
#: actually looking at. Measured unique WITHIN the base-terminal set; the popups
#: carry it too, so it is never used to pick a client across the whole list.
CLIENT_FLAG_FOCUSED = "focused"

#: 🔴 G14. The ONLY directory tree this process will create or append a log
#: under. `--log-path` and `$SESSION_WRITE_LOG` are both caller-influenced and
#: both reachable from the one allowlisted Bash entry; without this they are an
#: unconstrained "create nested directories anywhere and append a line I shaped"
#: primitive. Resolved through `realpath` at CHECK time, so neither `..` nor a
#: symlink planted inside the root can point out of it.
LOG_ROOT = os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), ".claude")

DEFAULT_LOG_PATH = os.path.join(LOG_ROOT, "session-write.log")
LOG_PATH_ENV = "SESSION_WRITE_LOG"

#: The character whose trailing occurrence tmux EATS off an argv token — see the
#: measurement in the docstring. Named so the refusal and the check cannot drift.
TMUX_ARGV_SEPARATOR = sr.TMUX_SEPARATOR_CHAR

#: Characters that `send-keys -l` delivers as a real Enter (measured).
#: 🔴 NOT THE GATE — these two are non-printable, so `TEXT_IS_ALLOWED` already
#: refuses them. This tuple exists only to give them a message that names the
#: hazard. Deleting it weakens the DIAGNOSIS, never the refusal; that is the
#: whole point of replacing the denylist, and a test pins it.
SUBMITTING_CHARS = ("\n", "\r")

#: 🔴 G13. The `--text` ALLOWLIST. Every codepoint not permitted here is refused
#: by number — see measurement (c): a Ctrl-O executed a pane's buffer with no
#: Enter sent, and the set of characters bound to an accept-line variant is a
#: property of the OPERATOR'S shell config, which this process cannot read. So
#: the question asked is "is this character safe to deliver", never "is this one
#: of the dangerous ones we thought of".
#:
#: `str.isprintable()` is False for exactly the Unicode categories that make a
#: byte a control or an invisible: Cc (NUL, `\n`, `\r`, `\x0f`, ESC, DEL), Cf,
#: Cs, Co (private use — the U+E000 that once rendered a diff identical to an
#: unedited line), Cn, Zl, Zp and Zs-except-ASCII-space. Tab is Cc and so must
#: be re-permitted explicitly; it is the one control character with an ordinary
#: typographic use and it cannot accept a line.
TEXT_EXTRA_ALLOWED = ("\t",)

#: 🔴 THE CATEGORIES THAT ARE REFUSED BUT ARE NOT CONTROL CHARACTERS — a
#: DIAGNOSIS set, never a gate (`TEXT_IS_ALLOWED` has already rejected every
#: member before this is consulted; a test pins that).
#:
#: Zs is every Unicode space EXCEPT the ASCII one (`" ".isprintable()` is True,
#: so U+0020 never reaches here); Zl/Zp are the line and paragraph separators;
#: Cf is the format characters — ZWSP, ZWJ, the soft hyphen, the bidi marks,
#: the BOM. Measured against the shipped predicate: U+00A0, U+202F, U+2003,
#: U+00AD, U+200B, U+200F, U+FEFF and every ZWJ emoji sequence land here.
#:
#: 🔴 THEY STAY REFUSED, AND THE ALLOWLIST IS NOT WIDENED. Two measurements say
#: so. (a) In a real bash pane on a private `-L` server, `touch<U+00A0>/tmp/f`
#: sent with `send-keys -l` was delivered verbatim and bash answered
#: `bash: touch\xa0/tmp/f: No such file or directory` — the payload became ONE
#: word and failed wearing a diagnosis about a PATH. That is the same class as
#: the trailing `;` this file already refuses: a payload silently corrupted
#: somewhere the caller will not look. (b) Normalising it to U+0020 behind the
#: caller's back is the mutation this file refuses on principle everywhere else.
#: So the fix is a REMEDY IN THE MESSAGE, not a wider gate.
TEXT_INVISIBLE_CATEGORIES = ("Cf", "Zl", "Zp", "Zs")


def TEXT_IS_ALLOWED(ch: str) -> bool:
    """🔴 THE allowlist predicate, ONE place. Every `--text` codepoint passes
    through exactly this function; there is no second copy to disagree with."""
    return ch in TEXT_EXTRA_ALLOWED or ch.isprintable()


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Exit codes. 0/2/3 are session-resolve's OWN vocabulary, imported rather than
# re-spelled; everything above is this module's.
#
# 🔴 AN EXIT CODE NAMES A CLASS OF REFUSAL, NOT AN INDIVIDUAL GATE. An earlier
# version of this comment claimed "every refusal has its own code so a caller
# can branch on WHICH gate stopped it", and that was simply false:
# `EXIT_AMBIGUOUS_CLIENT` covers five distinct refusals (no such client, base
# terminal, not a base terminal, no base terminal attached, two base terminals,
# two popups) and `EXIT_REMOTE_HOST` covers two (remote target, unknown local
# host). Widening the codes to one-per-refusal was rejected: a caller branching
# on "which client problem" wants a token, not a number it must keep a table for.
#
# The per-gate discriminator is `record["outcome"]` — a stable string token
# (`refused:base-terminal`, `refused:no-such-client`, …) emitted on EVERY run
# into the audit log and into `--json`. `OUTCOME_TOKENS` below enumerates them
# and a test pins that ledger against the tokens the source actually emits, so a
# new gate cannot arrive without a name.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
EXIT_OK = sr.EXIT_RESOLVED                  # 0
EXIT_AMBIGUOUS = sr.EXIT_AMBIGUOUS          # 2
EXIT_UNMATCHED = sr.EXIT_UNMATCHED          # 3
EXIT_REMOTE_HOST = 4
EXIT_SCREEN_NOT_YOURS = 5
EXIT_SHELL_EXEC = 6
EXIT_UNSENT_PROMPT = 7
EXIT_TARGET_VANISHED = 8
EXIT_AMBIGUOUS_CLIENT = 9
EXIT_TMUX_FAILED = 10
EXIT_BAD_TEXT = 11
EXIT_RESTORE_FAILED = 12
EXIT_BAD_LOG_PATH = 13

#: Every refusal code, so a test can assert they are pairwise distinct and that
#: none collides with session-resolve's three.
ALL_EXIT_CODES = (EXIT_OK, EXIT_AMBIGUOUS, EXIT_UNMATCHED, EXIT_REMOTE_HOST,
                  EXIT_SCREEN_NOT_YOURS, EXIT_SHELL_EXEC, EXIT_UNSENT_PROMPT,
                  EXIT_TARGET_VANISHED, EXIT_AMBIGUOUS_CLIENT,
                  EXIT_TMUX_FAILED, EXIT_BAD_TEXT, EXIT_RESTORE_FAILED,
                  EXIT_BAD_LOG_PATH)

#: 🔴 THE PER-GATE DISCRIMINATOR — see the exit-code note above. Exit codes name
#: a CLASS; these name the individual gate, and they are what a caller should
#: branch on. Pinned two-way by a test against the `outcome=` literals the
#: source actually emits, so neither a new gate without a token nor a token
#: nothing emits can survive the suite.
OUTCOME_TOKENS = (
    "refused:ambiguous",
    "refused:ambiguous-base-terminal",
    "refused:ambiguous-popup",
    "refused:bad-log-path",
    "refused:base-terminal",
    "refused:local-host-unknown",
    "refused:no-base-terminal",
    "refused:no-such-client",
    "refused:no-text",
    "refused:not-base-terminal",
    "refused:pane-vanished",
    "refused:remote-host",
    "refused:reverify-unreadable",
    "refused:screen-not-yours",
    "refused:shell-exec",
    "refused:text-invisible",
    "refused:text-not-printable",
    "refused:text-nul",
    "refused:text-submits",
    "refused:text-trailing-separator",
    "refused:unmatched",
    "refused:unsent-prompt",
    "refused:window-vanished",
    "failed:tmux",
    "noop:no-popups",
    "written:dismiss",
    "written:focus",
    "written:focus-restore-failed",
    "written:focus-stay",
    "written:send",
    "written:type",
)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# 🔴 REFUSAL MESSAGES, as whole-string templates.
#
# Pinned by tests as WHOLE NORMALISED STRINGS, not by keyword. A guard on a word
# is walkable by rewording — this repo has watched a two-word check be satisfied
# by a sentence's own static prose while neither computed slot was ever read. So
# every `{slot}` below is a COMPUTED value and the tests assert the rendered
# string in full. A cosmetic reword then fails the suite; that is the trade, and
# it is worth it for a machine-readable claim about what the tool refuses.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
MSG_FOCUS_NEEDS_FLAG = (
    "REFUSED: focus changes your screen.\n"
    "  re-run with --i-am-at-the-keyboard")

#: 🔴 A SEPARATE STRING FROM focus's, deliberately. They are two different
#: actions with two different consequences, and one shared message would let a
#: mutant move the gate between the verbs invisibly.
MSG_DISMISS_NEEDS_FLAG = (
    "REFUSED: dismiss detaches a client, which changes what is on your screen "
    "RIGHT NOW — and the 'popup' classification is made on the client's TERM, "
    "so a nested tmux attach is indistinguishable from an overlay and would be "
    "DETACHED rather than closed.\n"
    "  re-run with --i-am-at-the-keyboard")

MSG_REMOTE_HOST = (
    "REFUSED: {selector!r} resolved to host {target_host!r}, but this process "
    "writes to LOCAL tmux only (local host: {local_host!r}). Writing a remote "
    "address to local tmux would hit a DIFFERENT window that happens to share "
    "it. Run session-write on {target_host!r} instead.")

MSG_LOCAL_HOST_UNKNOWN = (
    "REFUSED: {selector!r} resolved to host {target_host!r}, but the LOCAL host "
    "name could not be determined ({local_host!r}), so locality cannot be "
    "proven. Refusing rather than assuming the target is local.")

MSG_AMBIGUOUS = (
    "REFUSED: {selector!r} matched {count} targets — refusing to pick one to "
    "write to. Disambiguate with an address (session:@window_id): {candidates}")

MSG_UNMATCHED = (
    "REFUSED: {selector!r} matched no target, so there is nothing to write to. "
    "{reason}")

MSG_VANISHED = (
    "REFUSED: {address} resolved, but it is GONE from tmux as of the check "
    "immediately before the write ({detail}). Nothing was written.")

MSG_BASE_TERMINAL = (
    "REFUSED: client {tty} on session {session!r} is the BASE TERMINAL, not a "
    "popup. Detaching it would drop the operator out of tmux entirely.")

#: 🔴 The MIRROR of MSG_BASE_TERMINAL, and the gap it closes: `focus` steers a
#: client with `switch-client -c`, and `--client <popup tty>` was ACCEPTED
#: because naming a client explicitly skipped the base-terminal derivation
#: entirely. Steering an overlay is not raising a window on the operator's
#: screen — the overlay is transient and the screen underneath never moves.
MSG_NOT_BASE_TERMINAL = (
    "REFUSED: client {tty} on session {session!r} is a POPUP, not the base "
    "terminal. focus steers the screen the operator is looking at; pointing it "
    "at an overlay moves a transient client and leaves the real screen "
    "untouched. Name a base terminal, or omit --client to let focus derive it.")

MSG_NO_SUCH_CLIENT = (
    "REFUSED: no client is attached at {tty}. Attached clients: {attached}")

MSG_AMBIGUOUS_POPUP = (
    "REFUSED: {count} popup clients are attached and none was named — refusing "
    "to guess which one is covering the screen. Re-run with --client TTY: "
    "{candidates}")

MSG_NO_POPUPS = (
    "dismiss: NOTHING TO DISMISS — no popup client is attached, so no overlay "
    "is covering {address}. Exit 0: the requested end state already holds.")

MSG_NO_BASE_TERMINAL = (
    "REFUSED: no base-terminal client is attached, so there is no screen to "
    "change. Attached clients: {attached}")

MSG_AMBIGUOUS_BASE_TERMINAL = (
    "REFUSED: {count} base-terminal clients are {qualifier} and none was named "
    "— refusing to guess which screen is the operator's. Re-run with "
    "--client TTY: {candidates}")

MSG_SHELL_EXEC = (
    "REFUSED: send presses Enter, and {address} has runtime {runtime} — not one "
    "of the agent runtimes ({agents}), so Enter would EXECUTE in a shell rather "
    "than submit a prompt. A null runtime means UNRECORDED, not 'shell', and is "
    "refused the same way. Re-run with --allow-shell-exec if that is intended.")

MSG_UNSENT_PROMPT = (
    "REFUSED: {address} already holds {length} character(s) of UNSENT text the "
    "operator typed and never sent; writing would CONCATENATE onto it. Re-run "
    "with --append to write onto it anyway. (The text itself is deliberately "
    "not printed.)")

#: 🔴 Bare `send` has a DIFFERENT consequence and so gets a different sentence:
#: nothing is concatenated, the operator's half-finished sentence is SUBMITTED
#: exactly as they left it. Sharing one message across the two shapes would make
#: a mutant that moves the gate between them invisible to a whole-string check.
MSG_UNSENT_PROMPT_SUBMIT = (
    "REFUSED: {address} holds {length} character(s) of UNSENT text the operator "
    "typed and never sent, and send with no --text would SUBMIT exactly that — "
    "a human's half-finished sentence, dispatched unread and unedited. Re-run "
    "with --append to submit it anyway. (The text itself is deliberately not "
    "printed.)")

MSG_TEXT_SUBMITS = (
    "REFUSED: --text contains {name} at offset {offset}, which tmux "
    "`send-keys -l` delivers as a real Enter — measured, it SUBMITS. Split the "
    "text and use send for each line instead.")

MSG_TEXT_TRAILING_SEP = (
    "REFUSED: --text ends with {sep!r}, which tmux EATS off the argv token as a "
    "command separator — measured, the pane receives the text WITHOUT it and "
    "the call still reports success. Append a space to write it literally.")

MSG_TEXT_NUL = (
    "REFUSED: --text contains a NUL byte at offset {offset}; it cannot be "
    "passed through an argv.")

#: 🔴 THE REMEDY MESSAGE, and why a "non-printable character" headline was the
#: wrong words for a SPACE.
#:
#: Measured against the shipped predicate: NBSP (U+00A0), narrow NBSP (U+202F),
#: em space (U+2003), soft hyphen, ZWSP, RLM and every ZWJ emoji sequence are
#: all refused — and NBSP is what a web page or a model's answer puts in text
#: the operator then pastes. The old message headlined it "the non-printable
#: character", which for a space reads as nonsense, and unlike its three
#: siblings it offered no next step. Both halves are the complaint; the gate
#: itself is correct and is NOT widened (see TEXT_INVISIBLE_CATEGORIES).
MSG_TEXT_INVISIBLE = (
    "REFUSED: --text contains {name} (U+{codepoint}, Unicode category "
    "{category}) at offset {offset}. That is not the ASCII space — it is an "
    "invisible or look-alike character, and it is REFUSED rather than silently "
    "replaced for the same reason a trailing {sep!r} is: MEASURED in a real "
    "bash pane, `touch<U+00A0>/tmp/f` was delivered verbatim and the shell "
    "answered `No such file or directory` for the whole thing as ONE word — a "
    "corrupted payload failing later, wearing a diagnosis about a path. "
    "REMEDY: replace it with an ordinary space (U+0020), or delete it, then "
    "re-run. If you pasted this out of a web page or a model's answer, that is "
    "where it came from.")

#: 🔴 G13's own refusal — the one that is reached by every codepoint the three
#: named messages above do NOT cover. It reports the codepoint BY NUMBER because
#: the offending character is by definition one that cannot be displayed.
MSG_TEXT_NOT_PRINTABLE = (
    "REFUSED: --text contains the non-printable character U+{codepoint} "
    "(Unicode category {category}) at offset {offset}. --text is validated "
    "against an ALLOWLIST of printable characters plus tab, not a denylist of "
    "the ones we thought of: MEASURED, `send-keys -l` of a payload ending in "
    "U+000F (Ctrl-O, readline operate-and-get-next, a bash default) EXECUTED "
    "the pane's buffer with no Enter sent. Which control characters accept a "
    "line is a property of your shell's keymap, which this tool cannot read.")

MSG_BAD_LOG_PATH = (
    "REFUSED: the log path {path!r} resolves to {resolved!r}, which is outside "
    "{root!r}. session-write CREATES missing directories at this path and "
    "APPENDS a JSON line shaped by its own arguments to it, so an unconstrained "
    "path is a write primitive rather than an audit trail. Nothing was "
    "resolved and nothing was written.")

MSG_TYPE_NEEDS_TEXT = (
    "REFUSED: type requires --text. To press Enter without typing anything, "
    "use send with no --text.")

MSG_TMUX_FAILED = (
    "FAILED: tmux {subcommand} exited {rc}: {stderr}")

MSG_RESTORE_FAILED = (
    "🔴 RESTORE FAILED — the screen is NOT where the operator left it. "
    "{detail}")

#: 🔴 Printed by every focus result, success or failure. The restore covers two
#: tmux dimensions and NOT the window manager, and an operator reading only the
#: happy path must still learn that.
MSG_FOCUS_SCOPE = (
    "scope: tmux only — the client's session and the session's active window. "
    "The i3 workspace is NOT changed and NOT restored by this tool.")


class WriteVerbViolation(RuntimeError):
    """Raised when a tmux subcommand outside the allowlist reaches the seam."""


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# G4 — the subprocess seam
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def _assert_allowed(argv) -> None:
    """🔴 Structural enforcement of the WRITE contract, mirroring
    `session-resolve._assert_read_only` in the other direction.

    Reuses session-resolve's `_split_on_separators` rather than re-deriving the
    welded-`;` handling: that function carries a measurement (tmux 3.7b really
    does start a second command on a `;` glued to the previous token) and a
    stated limit, and a private second copy would drift from both.
    """
    argv = list(argv)
    if not argv or os.path.basename(argv[0]) != "tmux":
        return
    for segment in sr._split_on_separators(argv[1:]):
        for token in segment:
            if token.startswith("-"):
                continue
            if token not in TMUX_ALLOWED_SUBCOMMANDS:
                raise WriteVerbViolation(
                    f"session-write refuses tmux command {token!r}; allowed: "
                    f"{', '.join(TMUX_ALLOWED_SUBCOMMANDS)}")
            break       # the rest of THIS segment is that command's arguments


def _default_runner(argv, timeout: int = 20):
    """Run argv, return (rc, stdout, stderr). Never raises on non-zero rc."""
    _assert_allowed(argv)
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True,
                              timeout=timeout, check=False)
    except FileNotFoundError as exc:
        return 127, "", f"not found: {exc}"
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return 124, "", f"timed out after {timeout}s"
    except OSError as exc:  # pragma: no cover - defensive
        return 1, "", str(exc)
    return proc.returncode, proc.stdout, proc.stderr


@dataclass
class WriteSources:
    """Every impure input, injectable — the whole suite runs with all of them
    supplied, so no test can reach the operator's tmux, registry or log.

    🔴 Every default resolves at CALL time, never as a field default. A
    dataclass field default is bound once at class-creation time, which is how
    session-resolve's `registry_dir` outran a monkeypatch and read 48 live
    records inside a suite that believed itself hermetic.
    """
    #: None -> `_default_runner` at call time.
    runner: Optional[Callable[..., Any]] = None
    #: The `session-resolve.Sources` used for resolution. None -> built from the
    #: CLI arguments in `main`.
    resolver_src: Optional[Any] = None
    #: None -> `$SESSION_WRITE_LOG`, else DEFAULT_LOG_PATH, resolved at call time.
    log_path: Optional[str] = None
    #: 🔴 G14's root, and DELIBERATELY NOT REACHABLE FROM argv OR THE ENVIRONMENT
    #: — `main` never sets it, so the only way to move it is to construct a
    #: `WriteSources` in-process, which only the test suite does. A root a caller
    #: could relocate is not a confinement. `None` -> `LOG_ROOT` at call time,
    #: never as a field default (see the class docstring's scar).
    log_root: Optional[str] = None
    #: None -> `resolve()`. Injectable so a test can pin a resolution without
    #: reconstructing four sources.
    resolver: Optional[Callable[..., Any]] = None
    #: None -> `datetime.now(timezone.utc)`.
    clock: Optional[Callable[[], Any]] = None
    #: Collected argv actually issued, in order. Populated by `run_tmux`.
    issued: list = field(default_factory=list)
    #: What the run resolved, for the audit record. `None` after a refusal that
    #: never got as far as resolving (G5), which is a state the record must be
    #: able to express rather than fake.
    last_target: Optional[dict] = None
    last_result: Optional[dict] = None

    def resolved_runner(self):
        return self.runner if self.runner is not None else _default_runner

    def resolved_log_root(self) -> str:
        return self.log_root if self.log_root is not None else LOG_ROOT

    def requested_log_path(self) -> str:
        """What the CALLER asked for — argv, then env, then the default. May be
        anywhere on the filesystem; `validate_log_path` is what decides."""
        if self.log_path is not None:
            return self.log_path
        env = os.environ.get(LOG_PATH_ENV)
        return env if env else DEFAULT_LOG_PATH

    def resolved_log_path(self) -> str:
        """🔴 The path actually WRITTEN, and it is inside the root by
        construction. `run` refuses the whole invocation when the requested path
        fails G14, but `main` still has to journal that refusal somewhere — and
        the one place it must not journal it is the path the caller was just
        refused. So this falls back to the default INSIDE the root rather than
        honouring a rejected request."""
        requested = self.requested_log_path()
        root = self.resolved_log_root()
        if validate_log_path(requested, root) is None:
            return requested
        return os.path.join(root, os.path.basename(DEFAULT_LOG_PATH))

    def resolved_resolver(self):
        return self.resolver if self.resolver is not None else sr.resolve

    def now_iso(self) -> str:
        if self.clock is not None:
            return str(self.clock())
        return _dt.datetime.now(_dt.timezone.utc).isoformat()


def run_tmux(ws: WriteSources, argv, dry_run: bool = False) -> tuple:
    """Issue one tmux command through the seam. -> (rc, stdout, stderr).

    Records the argv in `ws.issued` in BOTH modes, so a `--dry-run` test and a
    live test read the same list and a guard cannot be reachable in only one.
    A dry run still passes `_assert_allowed`: the allowlist is a property of the
    argv, not of whether it ran.
    """
    argv = list(argv)
    _assert_allowed(argv)
    ws.issued.append(argv)
    if dry_run:
        return 0, "", ""
    return ws.resolved_runner()(argv, timeout=20)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Outcome
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
@dataclass
class Outcome:
    code: int
    lines: list = field(default_factory=list)
    record: dict = field(default_factory=dict)

    @property
    def ok(self) -> bool:
        return self.code == EXIT_OK


def _refuse(code: int, message: str, *, outcome: str, **record) -> Outcome:
    """🔴 `outcome` IS REQUIRED AND KEYWORD-ONLY, and that is the whole point.

    It used to default to the bare token `"refused"`, and that fallback was
    INVISIBLE to its own ledger: `test_the_outcome_token_ledger_matches_what_
    the_SOURCE_actually_emits` filters the scanned tokens on `":" in t`, so a
    call site that forgot its token would emit a name the ledger was
    structurally unable to notice — a gate with no per-gate discriminator,
    passing the very test that exists to make that impossible. Every call site
    passed one anyway; nothing pinned that they must.

    Now a missing token is a `TypeError` at the call site rather than a silent
    downgrade, and `test_every_refusal_NAMES_its_gate` AST-pins that every call
    in this file really does pass one — the structural half, since a TypeError
    only fires on a path a test actually walks.
    """
    record = dict(record)
    record["outcome"] = outcome
    record["message"] = message
    return Outcome(code=code, lines=[message], record=record)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# G1 + G2 — resolve, then prove locality
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def resolve_local_target(selector: str, ws: WriteSources) -> tuple:
    """-> (target, result, refusal|None). G1 and G2, in that order.

    G2's authority for "which machine is this" is session-resolve's own
    `coverage.registry.local_host`, not `socket.gethostname()` — session-resolve
    already resolves that through an authority chain (explicit override ->
    session-manager's `local_host` -> a single `--host`), and a second answer
    computed a second way is a second thing that can disagree.
    """
    result = ws.resolved_resolver()(selector, ws.resolver_src)
    status = result.get("status")

    if status == sr.STATUS_AMBIGUOUS:
        candidates = ", ".join(
            str(c.get("address")) for c in result.get("candidates") or [])
        return None, result, _refuse(
            EXIT_AMBIGUOUS,
            MSG_AMBIGUOUS.format(selector=selector,
                                 count=result.get("candidate_count"),
                                 candidates=candidates or "(none listed)"),
            outcome="refused:ambiguous")

    if status != sr.STATUS_RESOLVED:
        return None, result, _refuse(
            EXIT_UNMATCHED,
            MSG_UNMATCHED.format(selector=selector,
                                 reason=result.get("reason") or ""),
            outcome="refused:unmatched")

    target = result["target"]
    local_host = (result.get("coverage") or {}).get("registry", {}).get(
        "local_host")
    target_host = target.get("host")

    if not local_host or local_host == sr.LOCAL_HOST_UNKNOWN:
        return None, result, _refuse(
            EXIT_REMOTE_HOST,
            MSG_LOCAL_HOST_UNKNOWN.format(selector=selector,
                                          target_host=target_host,
                                          local_host=local_host),
            outcome="refused:local-host-unknown")

    if target_host != local_host:
        return None, result, _refuse(
            EXIT_REMOTE_HOST,
            MSG_REMOTE_HOST.format(selector=selector, target_host=target_host,
                                   local_host=local_host),
            outcome="refused:remote-host")

    return target, result, None


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# G3 — re-verify IMMEDIATELY before the write
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
@dataclass
class Fresh:
    """tmux as it is RIGHT NOW, read in the same breath as the write."""
    windows: dict
    panes: list
    clients: list


def reverify(target: dict, ws: WriteSources, need_pane: bool) -> tuple:
    """-> (Fresh, refusal|None).

    🔴 This is the "re-verify at the moment you ACT" check, and it is deliberately
    NOT the resolution's own tmux read. Resolution consults four sources — one of
    them shells out to session-manager, which itself scans both hosts — so
    seconds pass between "this window exists" and "write to this window". The
    check is here, immediately before the destructive step, and every write is
    conditional on what IT returns rather than on what resolution saw.

    The fresh CLIENT list is returned too, because the same argument applies to
    the popup a `dismiss` is about to detach.
    """
    session = target.get("session")
    window_id = target.get("window_id")
    pane_id = target.get("pane_id")

    rc, out, err = run_tmux(
        ws, ["tmux", "list-windows", "-a", "-F", sr.WINDOW_FORMAT])
    if rc != 0:
        return None, _refuse(
            EXIT_TARGET_VANISHED,
            MSG_VANISHED.format(address=target.get("address"),
                                detail=f"tmux list-windows exited {rc}: "
                                       f"{(err or '').strip()[:120]}"),
            outcome="refused:reverify-unreadable")
    windows, _ = sr.parse_windows(out)

    if (session, window_id) not in windows:
        return None, _refuse(
            EXIT_TARGET_VANISHED,
            MSG_VANISHED.format(
                address=target.get("address"),
                detail=f"tmux no longer lists window {window_id} in session "
                       f"{session!r}"),
            outcome="refused:window-vanished")

    rc, out, err = run_tmux(
        ws, ["tmux", "list-panes", "-a", "-F", sr.PANE_FORMAT])
    if rc != 0:
        return None, _refuse(
            EXIT_TARGET_VANISHED,
            MSG_VANISHED.format(address=target.get("address"),
                                detail=f"tmux list-panes exited {rc}: "
                                       f"{(err or '').strip()[:120]}"),
            outcome="refused:reverify-unreadable")
    panes, _ = sr.parse_panes(out)

    if need_pane:
        live = {p["pane_id"] for p in panes
                if p["session"] == session and p["window_id"] == window_id}
        if pane_id is None or pane_id not in live:
            return None, _refuse(
                EXIT_TARGET_VANISHED,
                MSG_VANISHED.format(
                    address=target.get("address"),
                    detail=f"pane {pane_id!r} is not among the panes tmux now "
                           f"lists for that window ({', '.join(sorted(live)) or 'none'})"),
                outcome="refused:pane-vanished")

    rc, out, err = run_tmux(ws, ["tmux", "list-clients", "-F", sr.CLIENT_FORMAT])
    if rc != 0:
        return None, _refuse(
            EXIT_TARGET_VANISHED,
            MSG_VANISHED.format(address=target.get("address"),
                                detail=f"tmux list-clients exited {rc}: "
                                       f"{(err or '').strip()[:120]}"),
            outcome="refused:reverify-unreadable")
    clients, _ = sr.parse_clients(out)

    return Fresh(windows=windows, panes=panes, clients=clients), None


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Client selection
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def _client_summary(clients) -> str:
    return ", ".join(f"{c['tty']} ({c['session']}, "
                     f"{'popup' if c['popup'] else 'base'})"
                     for c in clients) or "none"


def _assert_not_base_terminal(client: dict) -> Optional[Outcome]:
    """🔴 G6, THE ONE PLACE. Every dismiss path funnels through this line
    immediately before the detach argv is built.

    It checks `base_terminal` rather than `not popup` even though
    `session-resolve.parse_clients` makes them exact complements, because the
    hazard has a name and the assertion should say it. If those two fields ever
    stop being complements, this still refuses the thing that drops the operator
    out of tmux.
    """
    if client.get("base_terminal"):
        return _refuse(
            EXIT_AMBIGUOUS_CLIENT,
            MSG_BASE_TERMINAL.format(tty=client.get("tty"),
                                     session=client.get("session")),
            outcome="refused:base-terminal")
    return None


def _assert_is_base_terminal(client: dict) -> Optional[Outcome]:
    """🔴 The MIRROR of `_assert_not_base_terminal`, and the ONE place focus's
    client is required to be a real screen.

    Checks `popup` rather than `not base_terminal` for the same reason its twin
    checks `base_terminal`: the hazard has a name and the assertion should say
    it. `parse_clients` makes the two exact complements today; if they ever stop
    being complements, each assertion still refuses the thing it is named for.
    """
    if client.get("popup"):
        return _refuse(
            EXIT_AMBIGUOUS_CLIENT,
            MSG_NOT_BASE_TERMINAL.format(tty=client.get("tty"),
                                         session=client.get("session")),
            outcome="refused:not-base-terminal")
    return None


def pick_named_client(clients, tty: str) -> tuple:
    """-> (client, refusal|None). Searches ALL attached clients, deliberately.

    🔴 Searching only the popups would make `_assert_not_base_terminal`
    UNREACHABLE — `--client <base tty>` would report "no such popup" and the
    guard that actually matters would never execute. A guard no case can reach
    is a guard no test can honestly pin.
    """
    for c in clients:
        if c.get("tty") == tty:
            return c, None
    return None, _refuse(
        EXIT_AMBIGUOUS_CLIENT,
        MSG_NO_SUCH_CLIENT.format(tty=tty, attached=_client_summary(clients)),
        outcome="refused:no-such-client")


def pick_base_terminal(clients, tty: Optional[str]) -> tuple:
    """-> (client, refusal|None). The screen the operator is looking at.

    🔴 Does NOT read `visibility.base_terminal` — see refutation (2): that field
    is `base_all[0]`, the first in list order, and TWO base terminals were
    measured. Disambiguation is on the `focused` flag, which was unique within
    the base-terminal set; when it is not unique (0 or 2+) this refuses rather
    than guessing which screen belongs to the human.
    """
    if tty is not None:
        client, refusal = pick_named_client(clients, tty)
        if refusal:
            return None, refusal
        # 🔴 THE ASSERTION MUST RUN ON THE EXPLICIT PATH TOO. It did not: naming
        # a client skipped straight past the base-terminal derivation, so
        # `focus --client <popup tty>` was accepted and `switch-client -c` was
        # pointed at an overlay. The mirror of G6, which had the same hole in
        # the other direction and is why `pick_named_client` searches ALL
        # clients rather than only the ones the verb expects.
        refusal = _assert_is_base_terminal(client)
        return (None, refusal) if refusal else (client, None)

    base = [c for c in clients if c.get("base_terminal")]
    if not base:
        return None, _refuse(
            EXIT_AMBIGUOUS_CLIENT,
            MSG_NO_BASE_TERMINAL.format(attached=_client_summary(clients)),
            outcome="refused:no-base-terminal")
    if len(base) == 1:
        return base[0], None

    focused = [c for c in base if CLIENT_FLAG_FOCUSED in (c.get("flags") or [])]
    if len(focused) == 1:
        return focused[0], None

    qualifier = ("attached and none is focused" if not focused
                 else "attached and focused")
    pool = base if not focused else focused
    return None, _refuse(
        EXIT_AMBIGUOUS_CLIENT,
        MSG_AMBIGUOUS_BASE_TERMINAL.format(
            count=len(pool), qualifier=qualifier,
            candidates=_client_summary(pool)),
        outcome="refused:ambiguous-base-terminal")


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# --text validation (the two measured tmux payload hazards)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def validate_text(text: str) -> Optional[Outcome]:
    """G13. An ALLOWLIST over `--text`, plus the trailing-separator refusal.

    🔴 THIS IS ONE SCAN, NOT FIVE CHECKS. Every codepoint is put to
    `TEXT_IS_ALLOWED` and the FIRST one that fails is refused. The four named
    messages below do not decide anything — they only choose better WORDS for a
    character the allowlist has already rejected. That ordering is the whole
    correction: the previous version asked "is this character one of the four we
    banned", so U+000F (Ctrl-O) passed and `type` executed arbitrary commands in
    any shell pane. `test_the_named_text_messages_are_DIAGNOSES_not_the_gate`
    pins that the allowlist alone still refuses every one of them, so a future
    edit that deletes a named branch weakens the message and not the guard.

    The offset in every refusal is COMPUTED from the scan — INCLUDING offset 0,
    which is the position the audited exploit itself occupied in one shape and
    which `test_a_disallowed_character_at_offset_ZERO_is_refused` now covers:
    every other fixture deliberately puts the character later, and until that
    test existed `enumerate(text[1:], 1)` survived the whole suite AND the
    committed sweep.
    """
    for idx, ch in enumerate(text):
        if TEXT_IS_ALLOWED(ch):
            continue
        # --- Rejected. Everything below only picks the wording. --------------
        if ch == "\x00":
            return _refuse(EXIT_BAD_TEXT, MSG_TEXT_NUL.format(offset=idx),
                           outcome="refused:text-nul")
        if ch in SUBMITTING_CHARS:
            name = "a newline" if ch == "\n" else "a carriage return"
            return _refuse(
                EXIT_BAD_TEXT,
                MSG_TEXT_SUBMITS.format(name=name, offset=idx),
                outcome="refused:text-submits")
        category = unicodedata.category(ch)
        if category in TEXT_INVISIBLE_CATEGORIES:
            return _refuse(
                EXIT_BAD_TEXT,
                MSG_TEXT_INVISIBLE.format(
                    name=unicodedata.name(ch, "an unnamed character"),
                    codepoint=f"{ord(ch):04X}", category=category, offset=idx,
                    sep=TMUX_ARGV_SEPARATOR),
                outcome="refused:text-invisible")
        return _refuse(
            EXIT_BAD_TEXT,
            MSG_TEXT_NOT_PRINTABLE.format(codepoint=f"{ord(ch):04X}",
                                          category=category,
                                          offset=idx),
            outcome="refused:text-not-printable")

    if text.endswith(TMUX_ARGV_SEPARATOR):
        return _refuse(
            EXIT_BAD_TEXT,
            MSG_TEXT_TRAILING_SEP.format(sep=TMUX_ARGV_SEPARATOR),
            outcome="refused:text-trailing-separator")
    return None


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# G14 — the log path
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def validate_log_path(path: str, root: str) -> Optional[Outcome]:
    """G14. -> a refusal, or None. Refuse a log path outside `root`.

    🔴 `realpath` BOTH SIDES before comparing. A prefix test on the raw strings
    is walkable by `..` and by a symlink planted inside the root, and comparing
    a resolved path against an unresolved root fails whenever the root itself
    contains a symlink (`~` behind one is ordinary).

    `commonpath`, never `startswith`: `~/.claudeX` starts with `~/.claude`.
    """
    resolved = os.path.realpath(path)
    real_root = os.path.realpath(root)
    try:
        inside = os.path.commonpath([resolved, real_root]) == real_root
    except ValueError:          # different drives / a relative-absolute mix
        inside = False
    if inside and resolved != real_root:
        return None
    return _refuse(
        EXIT_BAD_LOG_PATH,
        MSG_BAD_LOG_PATH.format(path=path, resolved=resolved, root=real_root),
        outcome="refused:bad-log-path")


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# G8 / G9 — the two write gates
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def gate_shell_exec(target: dict, allow: bool) -> Optional[Outcome]:
    """G8. Applies to `send` ONLY.

    🔴 Not because Enter is THE execution event — it is not, see measurement
    (c); a control character in `--text` is another one. It is because `send` is
    the verb that presses Enter, and G13 is what stops `type` from carrying an
    execution event of its own. Phrased carelessly ("Enter is the execution
    event") this sentence is the false premise that shipped the bypass, so it is
    spelled out here rather than abbreviated.
    """
    if allow:
        return None
    runtime = target.get("runtime")
    if runtime in AGENT_RUNTIMES:
        return None
    return _refuse(
        EXIT_SHELL_EXEC,
        MSG_SHELL_EXEC.format(address=target.get("address"),
                              runtime=repr(runtime),
                              agents=", ".join(AGENT_RUNTIMES)),
        outcome="refused:shell-exec")


def gate_unsent_prompt(target: dict, append: bool,
                       submitting_only: bool = False) -> Optional[Outcome]:
    """G9. 🔴 The prompt's CONTENT never leaves this function — only its length.

    `submitting_only` selects the wording for bare `send`, where the operator's
    text is SUBMITTED rather than written onto. Same gate, same token, same exit
    code — a different consequence deserves a different sentence.
    """
    if append:
        return None
    prompt = target.get("unsent_prompt")
    if not prompt:
        return None
    template = MSG_UNSENT_PROMPT_SUBMIT if submitting_only else MSG_UNSENT_PROMPT
    return _refuse(
        EXIT_UNSENT_PROMPT,
        template.format(address=target.get("address"), length=len(prompt)),
        outcome="refused:unsent-prompt")


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# tmux write helpers
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def _tmux_failed(argv, rc, stderr) -> Outcome:
    return _refuse(
        EXIT_TMUX_FAILED,
        MSG_TMUX_FAILED.format(subcommand=argv[1], rc=rc,
                               stderr=(stderr or "").strip() or "(no stderr)"),
        outcome="failed:tmux")


def _issue(ws: WriteSources, argv, dry_run: bool) -> Optional[Outcome]:
    rc, _out, err = run_tmux(ws, argv, dry_run=dry_run)
    if rc != 0:
        return _tmux_failed(argv, rc, err)
    return None


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# The verbs
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def verb_dismiss(target, ws, fresh, args) -> Outcome:
    """Detach the POPUP client overlaying the screen.

    🔴 `covered` IS NOT THE PRECONDITION. Measured, the brief's own target is
    `visible=False, covered=False` with two popups attached — `covered` is only
    true when the base terminal is showing the target AND a popup is up, so
    gating dismiss on it would refuse in exactly the state the operator is in.
    Dismiss's job is "take the overlay off the screen"; the selector says which
    target the operator is trying to get back to, and it is resolved (rather
    than ignored) so the verb obeys G1/G2/G3 like every other write.

    Zero popups -> EXIT_OK and NO write. The requested end state already holds,
    and a caller that retries on non-zero would loop forever against a machine
    that is already correct.
    Two or more -> REFUSED. The measured live state is two, and picking one is
    a coin flip on which of the operator's overlays disappears.
    """
    popups = [c for c in fresh.clients if c.get("popup")]

    if args.client is not None:
        client, refusal = pick_named_client(fresh.clients, args.client)
        if refusal:
            return refusal
    elif not popups:
        msg = MSG_NO_POPUPS.format(address=target.get("address"))
        return Outcome(code=EXIT_OK, lines=[msg],
                       record={"outcome": "noop:no-popups", "message": msg,
                               "popups_attached": 0})
    elif len(popups) > 1:
        return _refuse(
            EXIT_AMBIGUOUS_CLIENT,
            MSG_AMBIGUOUS_POPUP.format(count=len(popups),
                                       candidates=_client_summary(popups)),
            outcome="refused:ambiguous-popup")
    else:
        client = popups[0]

    refusal = _assert_not_base_terminal(client)
    if refusal:
        return refusal

    argv = ["tmux", "detach-client", "-t", client["tty"]]
    failed = _issue(ws, argv, args.dry_run)
    if failed:
        return failed

    # 🔴 The result SAYS how the classification was made. "popup" is a statement
    # about the client's TERM (`session-resolve.POPUP_TERM_PREFIX`), not proof
    # of a `display-popup` overlay — see G6. An operator whose session just
    # vanished needs to be able to read, from this line, that it may have been
    # detached rather than closed, and which client it was.
    msg = (f"dismissed: detached client {client['tty']} "
           f"(session {client['session']!r}, TERM {client.get('term')!r}, "
           f"classified as a popup on its TERM — not proof of a display-popup "
           f"overlay); {len(popups)} popup(s) were attached")
    return Outcome(code=EXIT_OK, lines=[msg],
                   record={"outcome": "written:dismiss", "message": msg,
                           "client_tty": client["tty"],
                           "popups_attached": len(popups)})


def verb_focus(target, ws, fresh, args) -> Outcome:
    """Raise the target on the operator's screen. G5 is enforced at the top of
    `run`, before resolution and before this is ever called, so this function
    cannot fire as a side effect of another verb.

    RECORD -> RAISE -> RESTORE, with the restore covering BOTH tmux dimensions.
    """
    client, refusal = pick_base_terminal(fresh.clients, args.client)
    if refusal:
        return refusal

    session = target["session"]
    window_id = target["window_id"]
    address = f"{session}:{window_id}"

    # ---- RECORD, before anything moves. -------------------------------------
    prev_session = client["session"]
    prev_window = None
    for (s, w), active in fresh.windows.items():
        if s == session and active:
            prev_window = w
            break

    lines = [MSG_FOCUS_SCOPE,
             f"recorded: client {client['tty']} was on session "
             f"{prev_session!r}; session {session!r} had active window "
             f"{prev_window or '(none — tmux listed no active window)'}"]

    record = {"outcome": "written:focus", "client_tty": client["tty"],
              "prev_session": prev_session, "prev_window": prev_window,
              "restored": False}

    # 🔴 What we ACTUALLY moved, recorded as we move it. The restore is driven
    # off these, not off the plan — RULES.md requires restoring "including on
    # failure", and the failure that matters is the one BETWEEN the two raise
    # commands: `select-window` lands, `switch-client` errors, and an early
    # return would leave the operator's session pointing at a window this tool
    # chose. Restoring off the plan instead of off what happened would also
    # issue a switch-client that never had a counterpart.
    moved_window = False
    moved_client = False
    raise_failure = None

    # ---- RAISE. Exactly once, no loop, no ping-pong. ------------------------
    rc, _o, err = run_tmux(ws, ["tmux", "select-window", "-t", address],
                           dry_run=args.dry_run)
    if rc != 0:
        raise_failure = _tmux_failed(["tmux", "select-window"], rc, err)
    else:
        moved_window = (prev_window is not None and prev_window != window_id)
        if prev_session != session:
            rc, _o, err = run_tmux(ws, ["tmux", "switch-client", "-c",
                                        client["tty"], "-t", session],
                                   dry_run=args.dry_run)
            if rc != 0:
                raise_failure = _tmux_failed(["tmux", "switch-client"], rc, err)
            else:
                moved_client = True

    if raise_failure is None:
        lines.append(f"raised: {address} on client {client['tty']}")
    else:
        lines.append(f"raise FAILED: {raise_failure.lines[0]}")

    if args.stay and raise_failure is None:
        lines.append("--stay: NOT restoring. The operator is left on "
                     f"{address}.")
        record["outcome"] = "written:focus-stay"
        return Outcome(code=EXIT_OK, lines=lines, record=record)

    # ---- RESTORE. Runs on the success path AND on a half-completed raise. ---
    problems = []
    if moved_window:
        rc, _o, err = run_tmux(ws, ["tmux", "select-window", "-t",
                                    f"{session}:{prev_window}"],
                               dry_run=args.dry_run)
        if rc != 0:
            problems.append(f"could not restore session {session!r} to its "
                            f"previous active window {prev_window} "
                            f"(tmux select-window exited {rc}: "
                            f"{(err or '').strip() or 'no stderr'})")
    elif prev_window is None and raise_failure is None:
        # We changed the session's active window but never measured what it was,
        # so there is nothing to put back. Say so — an unrestorable dimension is
        # a restore failure, not a silent success.
        problems.append(f"the previous active window of session {session!r} "
                        f"was never measured, so it could not be restored")
    if moved_client:
        rc, _o, err = run_tmux(ws, ["tmux", "switch-client", "-c",
                                    client["tty"], "-t", prev_session],
                               dry_run=args.dry_run)
        if rc != 0:
            problems.append(f"could not return client {client['tty']} to "
                            f"session {prev_session!r} (tmux switch-client "
                            f"exited {rc}: {(err or '').strip() or 'no stderr'})")

    record["moved_window"] = moved_window
    record["moved_client"] = moved_client

    if problems:
        detail = "; ".join(problems)
        lines.append(MSG_RESTORE_FAILED.format(detail=detail))
        record["outcome"] = "written:focus-restore-failed"
        record["restore_problems"] = problems
        record["message"] = detail
        return Outcome(code=EXIT_RESTORE_FAILED, lines=lines, record=record)

    if raise_failure is not None:
        # The raise failed and everything it had managed to move is back.
        raise_failure.record.update(record)
        raise_failure.record["outcome"] = "failed:tmux"
        raise_failure.record["restored"] = True
        return Outcome(code=raise_failure.code, lines=lines,
                       record=raise_failure.record)

    lines.append(f"restored: client {client['tty']} back on session "
                 f"{prev_session!r}, session {session!r} back on window "
                 f"{prev_window}")
    record["restored"] = True
    return Outcome(code=EXIT_OK, lines=lines, record=record)


def verb_type(target, ws, fresh, args) -> Outcome:
    """Literal text, NOT submitted.

    🔴 `validate_text` IS THE ONLY REASON THIS VERB IS SAFE, and the only reason
    it is exempt from G8. "No Enter" is not a property of `send-keys -l` — an
    earlier version of this docstring claimed it was, and measurement (c)
    refuted it: a Ctrl-O in the payload executed the pane's buffer with no Enter
    sent, which made this verb an arbitrary-command executor in any shell pane.
    What makes "not submitted" true is the G13 ALLOWLIST refusing every
    codepoint that is not printable-or-tab. Weaken that and this verb becomes a
    shell again — the two are one mechanism, not a guard and a nicety.
    """
    refusal = validate_text(args.text)
    if refusal:
        return refusal
    refusal = gate_unsent_prompt(target, args.append)
    if refusal:
        return refusal

    pane = target["pane_id"]
    failed = _issue(ws, ["tmux", "send-keys", "-t", pane, "-l", "--",
                         args.text], args.dry_run)
    if failed:
        return failed
    msg = (f"typed: {len(args.text)} character(s) into pane {pane} "
           f"({target.get('address')}); NOT submitted")
    return Outcome(code=EXIT_OK, lines=[msg],
                   record={"outcome": "written:type", "message": msg,
                           "pane_id": pane, "text": args.text,
                           "submitted": False})


def verb_send(target, ws, fresh, args) -> Outcome:
    """SUBMIT.

    With `--text`: type that text, THEN press Enter — two separate tmux argv, so
    the submit is an auditable act rather than a character buried in a payload.
    Without `--text`: press Enter ONLY, submitting whatever is already sitting
    in the pane. Both shapes press Enter, so both pass through G8.

    🔴 AND BOTH SHAPES PASS THROUGH G9. The first version exempted bare `send`,
    reasoning that submitting text already in the pane is its only purpose. But
    `unsent_prompt` is not "text already in the pane" in the neutral sense — it
    is specifically a HUMAN's half-finished sentence, and bare `send` submits it
    verbatim, unread, with no agent text involved at all. That is G9's exact
    hazard reached by a shorter route, and the exemption made the shorter route
    the UNGUARDED one. `--append` acknowledges it, as it does on the --text path.
    """
    if args.text is not None:
        refusal = validate_text(args.text)
        if refusal:
            return refusal
    refusal = gate_shell_exec(target, args.allow_shell_exec)
    if refusal:
        return refusal
    refusal = gate_unsent_prompt(target, args.append,
                                 submitting_only=args.text is None)
    if refusal:
        return refusal

    pane = target["pane_id"]
    if args.text is not None:
        failed = _issue(ws, ["tmux", "send-keys", "-t", pane, "-l", "--",
                             args.text], args.dry_run)
        if failed:
            return failed

    failed = _issue(ws, ["tmux", "send-keys", "-t", pane, "Enter"],
                    args.dry_run)
    if failed:
        return failed

    what = (f"{len(args.text)} character(s) then Enter" if args.text is not None
            else "Enter only (submitting whatever the pane already held)")
    msg = f"sent: {what} to pane {pane} ({target.get('address')})"
    return Outcome(code=EXIT_OK, lines=[msg],
                   record={"outcome": "written:send", "message": msg,
                           "pane_id": pane, "text": args.text,
                           "submitted": True})


#: Which verbs address a PANE (and so need the pane to survive G3), and which
#: address a CLIENT. Pinned by a test against the parser's own verb list so a
#: fifth verb cannot arrive unclassified.
PANE_VERBS = ("type", "send")
CLIENT_VERBS = ("dismiss", "focus")
ALL_VERBS = CLIENT_VERBS + PANE_VERBS

#: 🔴 G5's ledger: the verbs that change what a human is looking at RIGHT NOW,
#: each mapped to its OWN refusal wording. A dict rather than a tuple precisely
#: so the two messages cannot be shared — a mutant that swaps them is visible to
#: the whole-string assertions, and one shared message would make the swap
#: invisible. Pinned against `CLIENT_VERBS` by a test: every client-directed
#: verb issues `detach-client`, `select-window` or `switch-client`, and all
#: three change the screen, so the two sets must stay equal.
SCREEN_VERBS = {"focus": MSG_FOCUS_NEEDS_FLAG,
                "dismiss": MSG_DISMISS_NEEDS_FLAG}

_VERB_FUNCS = {"dismiss": verb_dismiss, "focus": verb_focus,
               "type": verb_type, "send": verb_send}


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# G10 — the log
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def log_record(ws: WriteSources, record: dict) -> Optional[str]:
    """Append one JSON line. -> an error string, or None.

    🔴 NEVER raises and never changes a verb's exit code. A wrapper that refuses
    to act because it cannot journal is a worse failure than the missing line;
    the problem goes to stderr where the operator sees it.
    """
    path = ws.resolved_log_path()
    try:
        parent = os.path.dirname(path)
        if parent:
            os.makedirs(parent, exist_ok=True)
        with open(path, "a", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            fh.write(json.dumps(record, sort_keys=True, default=str) + "\n")
    except OSError as exc:
        return f"session-write: could not write log {path}: {exc}"
    return None


def build_record(ws, args, target, result, outcome) -> dict:
    """The audit record. 🔴 `unsent_prompt` CONTENT is never included — only its
    presence and length. This repo is PUBLIC and that string is captured text
    off the operator's screen."""
    prompt = (target or {}).get("unsent_prompt")
    record = {
        "ts": ws.now_iso(),
        "verb": args.verb,
        "selector": args.selector,
        "dry_run": bool(args.dry_run),
        "resolve_status": (result or {}).get("status"),
        "target": None,
        "unsent_prompt_present": bool(prompt),
        "unsent_prompt_length": len(prompt) if prompt else 0,
        "tmux_argv_issued": [list(a) for a in ws.issued],
        "exit_code": outcome.code,
    }
    if target:
        record["target"] = {
            k: target.get(k) for k in
            ("host", "session", "window_id", "window_index", "pane_id",
             "address", "codename", "hotkey", "runtime", "label")
        }
    record.update(outcome.record)
    # 🔴 A DRY RUN MUST NOT LOG AS A WRITE. `outcome` is what a reader greps the
    # audit log for, and `written:type` beside `dry_run: true` invites exactly
    # the misreading the log exists to prevent — two fields that must be read
    # together are one field that can be read wrong.
    if args.dry_run and str(record.get("outcome", "")).startswith("written:"):
        record["outcome"] = "dry-run:" + record["outcome"].split(":", 1)[1]
    return record


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# CLI
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def build_parser() -> argparse.ArgumentParser:
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="session-write",
        description="Write to ONE resolved tmux target. Every payload is "
                    "validated here; the selector is re-resolved at write time.")
    p.add_argument("verb", choices=ALL_VERBS)
    p.add_argument("selector",
                   help="the SAME selector vocabulary session-resolve accepts "
                        "(codename, hotkey, session:index, session:@window_id, "
                        "@window_id, harness name, cwd). A raw tmux address is "
                        "re-resolved, never passed through.")
    p.add_argument("--text", default=None,
                   help="literal text (type: required; send: optional)")
    p.add_argument("--append", action="store_true",
                   help="write to — or, for bare send, SUBMIT — a pane that "
                        "holds UNSENT operator text")
    p.add_argument("--allow-shell-exec", action="store_true",
                   help="send: press Enter even though the runtime is not one "
                        "of " + ", ".join(AGENT_RUNTIMES))
    p.add_argument("--i-am-at-the-keyboard", dest="at_keyboard",
                   action="store_true",
                   help="focus and dismiss: required acknowledgement that this "
                        "changes the operator's screen")
    p.add_argument("--stay", action="store_true",
                   help="focus: do NOT restore the previous session/window")
    p.add_argument("--client", default=None, metavar="TTY",
                   help="name the client explicitly (e.g. /dev/pts/2) when "
                        "dismiss or focus would otherwise be ambiguous")
    p.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true",
                   help="run every guard and the re-verify, print the tmux "
                        "argv that WOULD be issued, write nothing")
    p.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--log-path", default=None,
                   help="the JSONL audit log. MUST resolve inside " + LOG_ROOT +
                        " (G14); anything else is refused before the selector "
                        "is even resolved")
    p.add_argument("--host", default=sr.HOST_ALL,
                   choices=(sr.HOST_ALL, *sr.HOST_NAMES))
    p.add_argument("--registry-dir", default=None)
    p.add_argument("--slot-table", default=None)
    return p


def run(args, ws: WriteSources) -> Outcome:
    """The whole pipeline, injectable end to end. `main` only does argv, I/O
    and the exit code."""
    # G14 FIRST OF ALL. The log write happens in `main` AFTER this returns, so a
    # refusal here is still journalled — but to the DEFAULT path, never to the
    # one being refused. Ordering it ahead of G5 is deliberate: a bad log path
    # is a property of the invocation, not of the verb, and every verb reaches
    # the log.
    refusal = validate_log_path(ws.requested_log_path(), ws.resolved_log_root())
    if refusal:
        return refusal

    # G5 — before resolution, so a refused screen-changing verb cannot even read
    # the world, and cannot be reached by any other verb's code path.
    #
    # 🔴 BOTH screen verbs, and with SEPARATE messages. `dismiss` was outside
    # this gate in the first version; detaching a client changes what the
    # operator is looking at just as raising a window does, and G6 records why
    # "it is only an overlay" is not something this tool can prove.
    if args.verb in SCREEN_VERBS and not args.at_keyboard:
        return _refuse(EXIT_SCREEN_NOT_YOURS, SCREEN_VERBS[args.verb],
                       outcome="refused:screen-not-yours")

    if args.verb == "type" and args.text is None:
        return _refuse(EXIT_BAD_TEXT, MSG_TYPE_NEEDS_TEXT,
                       outcome="refused:no-text")

    target, result, refusal = resolve_local_target(args.selector, ws)
    ws.last_target, ws.last_result = target, result
    if refusal:
        return refusal

    fresh, refusal = reverify(target, ws, need_pane=args.verb in PANE_VERBS)
    if refusal:
        return refusal

    return _VERB_FUNCS[args.verb](target, ws, fresh, args)


def main(argv=None, ws: Optional[WriteSources] = None) -> int:
    """🔴 `ws` is a TEST SEAM, and it is the only way the log path, the tmux
    runner and the resolver can be reached from a test. Without it `main` was
    the one function the suite could not exercise hermetically — so the argv
    parsing, the record assembly and the log write would have been covered only
    by reading them, which is not coverage."""
    args = build_parser().parse_args(argv)

    if ws is None:
        ws = WriteSources(log_path=args.log_path)
    elif args.log_path is not None:
        ws.log_path = args.log_path
    if ws.resolver_src is None:
        kwargs = {"host": args.host, "want_pr": False}
        if args.registry_dir is not None:
            kwargs["registry_dir"] = args.registry_dir
        if args.slot_table is not None:
            kwargs["slot_table_path"] = args.slot_table
        ws.resolver_src = sr.Sources(**kwargs)

    # Resolution runs through session-resolve's OWN runner, which enforces its
    # OWN read-only allowlist at its own seam. Nothing here relaxes that.
    outcome = run(args, ws)

    record = build_record(ws, args, ws.last_target, ws.last_result, outcome)
    err = log_record(ws, record)
    if err:
        print(err, file=sys.stderr)

    if args.json:
        print(json.dumps(record, indent=2, sort_keys=True, default=str))
    else:
        for line in outcome.lines:
            print(line)
        if args.dry_run:
            # A refusal issues no argv at all; printing an empty "would have
            # been issued" list reads as though something was withheld.
            if ws.issued:
                print("--dry-run: NOTHING was written. tmux argv that would "
                      "have been issued:")
                for a in ws.issued:
                    print("  " + " ".join(a))
            else:
                print("--dry-run: NOTHING was written, and nothing would have "
                      "been — the run was refused before any tmux argv was "
                      "built.")
    return outcome.code


if __name__ == "__main__":  # pragma: no cover
    sys.exit(main())
